WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:02.530
INTRO:
Teachers around the world generally despise

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:07.930
Wikipedia given that Wikipedia isn’t always
the most reliable or reputable source.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:11.709
With that being said though, I think students
around the world would agree that Wikipedia

00:00:11.709 --> 00:00:12.709
is a lifesaver.

00:00:12.709 --> 00:00:16.760
Whether you’re trying to look up a simple
equation or trying to cram for your fluid

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.520
mechanics test, Wikipedia has got you covered.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.900
And the truth is, while Wikipedia may not
have been the most accurate in its early days,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.780
nowadays, Wikipedia is pretty darn accurate.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.029
Moreover, most Wikipedia articles are really
good at citing all of their sources whether

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:34.110
that be textbooks or news publications.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:39.390
So, you can easily verify all the info on
Wikipedia, and cite the source material.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:45.330
And let’s not forget, Wikipedia is not only
free, but they don’t run any ads, or sponsorships,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.430
or affiliate marketing, or any of that.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:52.510
It’s basically completely run on donations
and unlike textbook publishers, Wikipedia

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.329
truly isn’t in it for the profit.

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:59.650
But, who even created this gem of a website
and where are they today?

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:05.229
JIMMY WALES:
Taking a look back, Wikipedia was founded

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:08.299
by two men the first of which being Jimmy
Wales.

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:11.799
Jimmy was born on August 7, 1966, in Huntsville
Alabama.

00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:16.779
Fun fact, he was born minutes before midnight,
so even though he was technically born on

00:01:16.779 --> 00:01:20.619
August 7th, his birth certificate ended up
saying August 8.

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:24.079
That must be confusing whenever someone asks
for your birthday.

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:28.569
Anyway, Jimmy’s father was a grocery store
manager, and his mother along with his grandmother

00:01:28.569 --> 00:01:30.539
ran a small private school.

00:01:30.539 --> 00:01:33.990
Jimmy and his siblings would all end up attending
this private school.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:38.369
From a very young age, Jimmy was a massive
fan of reading which is probably something

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:40.619
that most of us can’t relate with.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:45.109
When he was just 3 years old, his mother got
him a World Book Encylopedia, and as Jimmy

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:47.700
grew up, he ended up reading the book several
times.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.869
But, this quickly led him to a stumble upon
a shortcoming.

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:54.659
If something was wrong in the encyclopedia,
how would the information be updated?

00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:59.939
Of course, newer editions could just be printed
with the updated information, but encyclopedias

00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:03.469
were generally long-term investments that
cost well over $1000.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:08.940
So, people weren’t gonna buy new encyclopedias
on a regular basis, but they also wanted the

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.360
most up-to-date info.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.110
The most common solution for this shortfall
was stickers.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:18.080
Publishers would send out correction stickers
to Encylopedia owners, and owners could cover

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.610
up the original text with the updated sticker.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:25.270
Over the years, Jimmy stickered up his encyclopedia
which was his first experience in revising

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.340
an encyclopedia, but he wouldn’t revisit
this experience for decades.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.290
After elementary and middle school, Jimmy
attended a university preparatory school called

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:34.290
Randolph School.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:39.170
Apparently, the school was rather expensive
for his family, but they paid the bill anyway

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.460
because they highly valued education.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.840
The preparatory school allowed Jimmy to graduate
a bit early at 16, and he would end up attending

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.420
Auburn University where majored in finance.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.310
After his bachelor’s, Jimmy tried pursuing
a Ph.D. in finance from the University of

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:56.000
Alabama, but he ended up with a master’s
in finance instead.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.870
He gave the Ph.D. one more shot at Indiana
University, but he never wrote a doctoral

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.510
thesis which he claims was simply due to boredom.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.560
With all this education, it wouldn’t have
been surprising if Jimmy jumped straight into

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.000
the world of encyclopedias, but Jimmy actually
entered the world of finance.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.210
In 1994, he got a job at Chicago Options Associates
which was a futures and options trading firm.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:22.300
Despite his career in finance, Jimmy’s true
interest was always within the internet.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:27.080
He was a massive fan of a game called Multi-User
Dungeons and he regularly moderated various

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.460
online forums.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.990
In other words, he was the modern-day Discord
moderator.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:36.600
While Discord mods often get a lot of slack,
moderation was actually quite beneficial for

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.600
Jimmy.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.690
You see, it was while moderating an online
discussion group about philosophy that Jimmy

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:45.520
would run into Wikipedia’s other founder
Larry Sanger.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:50.650
LARRY SANGER:
Taking a look back once again, Larry Anger

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:54.460
was born on July 16, 1968, in Bellevue Washington.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.240
His father worked as a marine biologist while
his mother was a homemaker.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.690
When he was just 7, the family moved to a
rather unique location which was Anchorage,

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:03.690
Alaska.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.770
Despite the cool change of scenery, Larry’s
interests didn’t sway much.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:11.680
Like Jimmy, Larry was also a massive fan of
reading and especially learning about philosophy.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.410
Following his interests, Larry would end up
attending the same college as Steve Jobs,

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.799
Reed College, where he majored in philosophy.

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:22.099
Aside from his interest in philosophy, Larry
was also super intrigued by the internet.

00:04:22.099 --> 00:04:24.770
He wasn’t a gamer or an online memer though.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.180
He was primarily intrigued by the internet’s
potential to be used as a publishing outlet.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:33.210
You could reach millions if not tens of millions
of people through the internet without having

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:37.229
to spend millions yourself on publishing,
licensing, and distribution.

00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:41.180
Larry decided to put this theory to the test
during college by creating a discussion forum

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.379
that allowed students to meet with tutors
online.

00:04:43.379 --> 00:04:46.610
In other words, he basically created the modern-day
Chegg.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:51.610
So, you got the discord mod and the Chegg
founder, and these two would meet in 1994

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.599
when Larry subscribed to Jimmy’s mailing
list called Moderated Discussion of Objectivist

00:04:55.599 --> 00:04:57.729
Philosophy or MDOP.

00:04:57.729 --> 00:05:02.460
The two quickly became friends and had discussions
about life and philosophy here and there,

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.419
but neither of them had the idea of starting
a business together.

00:05:05.419 --> 00:05:10.759
So, Larry simply continued on in his educational
journey stacking on degree after degree.

00:05:10.759 --> 00:05:15.470
In 1995, Larry earned a Master’s in philosophy
from Ohio State University and he would turn

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:19.509
around and also get a Ph.D. in philosophy
from Ohio State in 2000.

00:05:19.509 --> 00:05:24.379
In the meantime, Larry also ran a website
called Sanger and Shannon’s Review of Y2K

00:05:24.379 --> 00:05:27.830
News Reports which was dedicated to discussing
the Year 2000 problem.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:31.729
If you’re not familiar with this, it was
basically the fear that computers would glitch

00:05:31.729 --> 00:05:36.870
out and have a bunch of errors as soon as
the date changed from 1999 to 2000.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:41.580
While there were some errors here and there
once we hit 2000, nothing catastrophic unfolded,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.620
and the Year 2000 problem ended up being a
big nothing burger.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:48.900
Larry’s site never really gained that much
popularity in the first place, but even if

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.510
it did, it wouldn’t have mattered much given
that its popularity would’ve fallen off

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:54.110
a cliff once 2000 hit.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:58.100
Clearly, Larry’s wasn’t having much success
with the internet, but the same could not

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:01.059
be said about Jimmy.

00:06:01.059 --> 00:06:04.760
NUPEDIA:
Unlike Larry who likely didn’t have too

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:09.330
much money to invest into internet projects,
Jimmy was stacked thanks to Wall Street.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:13.259
Jimmy had correctly speculated on fluctuations
in interest rates and foreign currencies for

00:06:13.259 --> 00:06:15.729
years which made him quite a bit of money.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:20.610
And seeing the insanely successful IPO of
Netscape in 1995, Jimmy knew that he had to

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.809
invest all this money into an internet project.

00:06:22.809 --> 00:06:27.729
Initially, he launched a search engine called
Bomis which was a guy-oriented search engine,

00:06:27.729 --> 00:06:29.610
but this never really took off.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:33.620
So, Jimmy decided to revisit his childhood
interest in encyclopedias.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:37.610
The idea was to create a free-to-use online
encyclopedia called Nupedia that made money

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.279
by displaying ads.

00:06:39.279 --> 00:06:44.250
For Jimmy, the accuracy of the content was
extremely important, so he wanted academic

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.930
experts to review all the content before it
was published on the website.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:52.930
And who better to hire as an academic expert
than his philosophy friend with a Ph.D., Larry.

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:57.520
In early 2000, Jimmy hired Larry as editor
in chief and together they launched Nupedia

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:58.800
in March of 2000.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.009
Nupedia didn’t just instantly blow up though.

00:07:02.009 --> 00:07:06.639
You see, while the information on Nupedia
was extremely accurate, no one wanted to contribute

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:09.620
to the site due to their extremely rigorous
approval process.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:13.749
Jimmy had hired a bunch of highly respected
professors to review articles on the side

00:07:13.749 --> 00:07:18.029
which was great for readers, but simply intimated
writers which makes sense.

00:07:18.029 --> 00:07:22.759
If you’re writing an article for the internet
for free, I’m sure you want it to be accurate,

00:07:22.759 --> 00:07:26.089
but it’s not like you want it to be graded
by university professors.

00:07:26.089 --> 00:07:30.330
So, very few people from the outside contributed
which meant that most of the articles came

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:32.180
from Jimmy and his team.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:35.960
Jimmy didn’t let this discourage him though,
he felt that Nupedia had great potential,

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:38.080
and he got to work on grinding out articles.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.779
But, this all changed when he started writing
an article about a Nobel Prize-winning economist

00:07:42.779 --> 00:07:44.520
named Robert C. Merton.

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:49.099
As ya’ll know, Jimmy had a master’s in
finance and had years of industry experience,

00:07:49.099 --> 00:07:52.319
yet he himself felt intimated to write about
Robert.

00:07:52.319 --> 00:07:56.320
He says that it felt like writing a doctoral
thesis, and he didn’t even wanna do that

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.839
during college when a Ph.D. was on the line.

00:07:58.839 --> 00:08:02.350
So, why in the world would anyone ever want
to do this during their free time.

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:07.160
It was clear to Jimmy and Larry that the current
model wouldn’t work, so they decided to

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.740
launch a more casual version of Nupedia called
Wikipedia.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:16.339
WIKIPEDIA:
Neither Jimmy nor Larry had very high expectations

00:08:16.339 --> 00:08:17.690
for Wikipedia.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.009
All of the professors that they worked with
dismissed the idea as something that would

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:22.340
never work.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:25.979
Jimmy and Larry themselves largely agreed
with this viewpoint, and they were simply

00:08:25.979 --> 00:08:30.839
trying to recruit more writers through Wikipedia,
but to all of their surprise, Wikipedia turned

00:08:30.839 --> 00:08:32.940
into much more than just that.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:38.510
Wikipedia’s first edit came out on January
14, 2001, and within just a couple of days,

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.570
the number of articles on Wikipedia outgrew
Nupedia.

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:45.500
Initially, Jimmy was super paranoid about
how Wikipedia would affect Nupedia’s brand

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:46.620
image.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:50.100
Apparently, he would wake up in the middle
of the night to read through new submissions

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:53.530
on Wikipedia and make sure that they were
of acceptable quality.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:57.290
In the meantime, Larry was having quite a
bit of fun with Wikipedia.

00:08:57.290 --> 00:09:01.750
He came up with the policy for the website
which included “Ignore all rules”, “Neutral

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:04.660
Point of View”, “No original research”,
and “Verifiability”.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:08.310
Ironically though, Jimmy and Larry would quickly
swap places.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:12.940
You see, while Larry was having a lot of fun
with Wikipedia, he never wanted it to replace

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:14.150
Nupedia.

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:19.100
On the other hand, while Jimmy highly prioritized
accuracy, at the core, he was a businessman,

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.630
and Wikipedia was clearly where the opportunity
was.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.630
So, as funding dried up following the dot-com
crash, Jimmy pulled the plug on Nupedia in

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:29.160
early 2002 and went all-in on Wikipedia.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:33.380
Jimmy no longer wanted to fund a job relating
to Nupedia and Larry was unwilling to switch

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:38.200
over to Wikipedia, so the duo would split
ways in March of 2002.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:42.590
Over the next year, Larry tried his best to
revive Nupedia by looking for buyers and even

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:44.450
trying to buy it himself.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:49.970
But when Nupedia’s servers crashed in September
of 2003, Larry knew that that was the end.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:55.250
Soon after, Jimmy tried to monetize Wikipedia
by displaying ads, but this was met with a

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:56.860
lot of backlash from the community.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:01.770
It was the community that made Wikipedia what
it was, so why should one organization or

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.020
person reap all the benefits.

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:07.210
Jimmy didn’t fight back much and gave in
pretty easily.

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:10.460
It seems like Jimmy’s main goal wasn’t
trying to make a lot of money but to create

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:15.810
something big and influential, and it was
clear to him that Wikipedia was exactly that.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:21.830
JIMMY &amp; LARRY TODAY:
In mid-2003, Jimmy launched a nonprofit organization

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:25.710
called the Wikimedia Foundation, and this
nonprofit would take over the daily operations

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.240
of Wikipedia.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:31.330
Over the next couple of years, Wikipedia would
crush milestone after milestone growing into

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:33.570
hundreds of thousands of articles.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:38.031
Jimmy served as the foundation’s official
chairman for a few years, but in 2006, he

00:10:38.031 --> 00:10:40.510
decided that the foundation no longer needed
him.

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:44.350
He does still have a special seat on the board
called the community founder’s seat, but

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:46.770
his role today is pretty hands-off.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.800
Since leaving Wikipedia, Jimmy has primarily
spent his time on other community-oriented

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:51.800
projects.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:56.310
In 2012, for instance, he was an advisor to
the UK government helping to make taxpayer-funded

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.140
research freely available on the internet.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:03.390
Similarly, in 2017, he launched an online
publication called WikiTribune which was dedicated

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.220
to fighting biased news.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:09.080
And in 2019, he launched an ad-free social
network called WikiTribune Social.

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:13.140
In the meantime, Larry has grown more and
more distant from Wikipedia.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:18.350
In fact, he has actually become a major critic
of Wikipedia and has commonly made statements

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:19.470
that discredit the site.

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:23.160
He also tried to launch a competitor to Wikipedia
called Citizendium in 2006.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:27.570
It drew on the community nature of Wikipedia
but also had more checks in place to ensure

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:28.570
credibility.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:33.080
Unfortunately, Citizendium never took off,
but Larry also never gave up.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:38.260
For example, between 2008 and 2010, he worked
on the WatchKnowLearn project which attempted

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:41.410
to create a repository of educational videos
for kids.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:45.810
Similarly, in 2013, he launched a crowdsourced
news portal called Infobitt.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:50.360
And in December 2017, he launched Everipedia
which was again a more credible version of

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.270
Wikipedia but based on the blockchain.

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:56.510
You just have to take one look at the all-time
graph of the everipedia token to see how that

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:57.810
turned out.

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:02.510
Despite all these failures, Larry still hasn’t
quit in trying to create an antithesis to

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:03.510
Wikipedia.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:06.610
In October of 2019, for instance, he launched
encylosphere.org.

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:10.190
At the end of the day, the truth is that most
of Larry’s criticisms about the credibility

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:12.690
of Wikipedia are true and fair.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:16.320
But it seems like Larry has never been able
to look past that and see the bright side

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.130
of Wikipedia which is truly quite a shame.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:23.010
Jimmy, however, was able to see Wikipedia
for exactly what it was, and I think he says

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:24.010
it best.

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:28.560
He estimates that Wikipedia is worth at least
$3 billion today, so by donating Wikipedia

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:31.980
to the foundation, you could say that he lost
out on $3 billion.

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:36.860
But, he also strongly believes that if he
didn’t donate Wikipedia, it wouldn’t be

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:37.890
worth $3 billion today.

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:42.760
So, donating the site will forever be the
dumbest and smartest decision of his life.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.910
Do you guys have a positive or negative opinion
on Wikipedia?

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:47.060
Comment that down below.

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:50.000
Also, drop a like if Wikipedia carried you
on an exam.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:54.710
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages,

00:12:54.710 --> 00:13:00.200
and consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

